 \begin{abstract}
-Knowledge Tracing (KT) models are typically evaluated with aggregate predictive metrics such as the area under the ROC curve (AUC) and accuracy. Such metrics can mask how a model behaves on sparse and cold-start Knowledge Components (KCs)---concepts with limited or no training-fold evidence---even though reliable probability estimates on these concepts are precisely what educational decision support depends on. Rather than proposing a new model, we treat KT evaluation as a reproducibility and diagnostic problem. We present a reproducible diagnostic protocol that extends standard KT evaluation. It combines learner-based and temporal splits, train-only KC-frequency stratification with an explicit strict cold-start group, and sample-size-aware reporting. Furthermore, it introduces per-stratum calibration analysis using the Expected Calibration Error (ECE), Brier-score decomposition, and reliability diagrams, supported by an operational leakage and predictive-sanity workflow (L1--L8). We instantiate the protocol on three datasets---ASSISTments 2012, Junyi Academy, and XES3G5M---with IRT (Item Response Theory), DKT (Deep Knowledge Tracing), and SimpleKT baselines. Under our experimental conditions, rather than a universal degradation in discrimination (AUC), we find that calibration susceptibility is a more informative vulnerability on sparse concepts. For instance, while some datasets like ASSISTments 2012 show a pronounced calibration degradation on less-frequent concepts, other datasets exhibit inverted or flat trends. This dataset-dependent calibration vulnerability highlights that aggregate metrics consistently obscure stratum-level reliability differences; the predictive-sanity channel additionally flagged a prediction--label misalignment that aggregate metrics alone would not reveal. Sparse-concept diagnostics are most informative when a dataset contains a meaningful low-frequency tail, those strata have sufficient evaluation support, and calibration reliability varies systematically with training evidence or deployment involves concept cold-start. Occupancy reporting remains useful even when that empirical priority is low. We provide an anonymized reproducibility artifact for peer review and will release the public version upon acceptance.
+Knowledge Tracing (KT) models are typically evaluated with aggregate predictive metrics such as the area under the ROC curve (AUC) and accuracy. These metrics can mask how a model behaves on sparse and cold-start Knowledge Components (KCs)---concepts with limited or no training-fold evidence---even though educational decision support often depends on the reliability of predicted probabilities. Rather than proposing a new model, we treat KT evaluation as a reproducibility and diagnostic problem. We present a diagnostic protocol that combines learner-based and temporal splits, train-only KC-frequency stratification with an explicit strict cold-start group, per-stratum calibration analysis via Expected Calibration Error (ECE), Brier-score decomposition, and reliability diagrams, sample-size-aware reporting, and an operational leakage and predictive-sanity workflow (L1--L8). We instantiate the protocol on ASSISTments 2012, Junyi Academy, and XES3G5M with IRT, DKT, and SimpleKT. Sparse training evidence does not universally degrade discrimination, but it can expose dataset-dependent calibration vulnerability. On ASSISTments 2012, SimpleKT ECE rises from $0.113$ on dense KCs to $0.225$ on sparse KCs (Limited flag, $N=403$); other datasets show flat or inverted stratum patterns. A controlled reduction of training rows for the same originally dense KCs increases within-KC ECE only in some dataset--model cells. We characterize the conditions associated with this vulnerability---sparse mass, evaluation support, and frequency--difficulty coupling---and provide reproducible diagnostics for identifying when it matters. Occupancy reporting remains useful even when the empirical priority of sparse-stratum claims is low. Channel L8 additionally flagged a prediction--label misalignment that aggregate metrics alone would not reveal. We provide an anonymized reproducibility artifact for peer review and will release the public version upon acceptance.
 \end{abstract}
 
